# Line endings: none converted, in either direction.
#
# The project is built on Linux (CI matrix, MSYS2, the headless plugin jobs) and
# on Windows.  `-text` makes git store the working-tree bytes and check out those
# same bytes: there is no `text=auto` sniffing and no `eol=` conversion on commit
# or on checkout.  So a file cannot differ between the two platforms because of
# git, and an unchanged file never shows up as rewritten.
#
# What the repository holds, then, is what everyone gets:
#
#   - LF everywhere: sources, CMake, shell scripts, the PowerShell script.
#   - CRLF in the batch files, and only there.  cmd.exe parses labels and `goto`
#     on its own line-ending rules and mis-handles a bare LF, so `*.bat` and
#     `*.cmd` are CRLF *in the repository*.  With no conversion configured, a
#     CRLF blob checks out as CRLF on every platform.  Stating them here beats
#     `eol=crlf` over an LF index: with conversion on, the cleaning filter's
#     output could never match a CRLF blob, so these two files reported as
#     modified on every `git status` in every checkout.
#
# Because nothing is converted, a stray CRLF in any other file stays in the
# commit.  Keep it out with an editor setting, not with git rewriting files.
* -text

# The one exception, stated so it is visible in `git check-attr` and
# `git ls-files --eol` rather than only in the comment above.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text

# Binary assets: never normalized, never treated as text by diff or merge.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.tif   binary
*.tiff  binary
*.bmp   binary
*.iwi   binary
*.hdr   binary
*.ico   binary
*.obj   binary
*.lib   binary
